  "strategy": "We propose to use a genetic algorithm (GA) based search to find the optimal sequence of coefficients. This approach is more suitable for exploring a large portion of the solution space systematically and escaping local minima effectively.\n\nThe algorithm will work as follows:\n\n1. Representation:\n   - We represent the sequence as a list of non-negative floats, with each element clamped between 0 and 1000.\n   - The sequence will have a fixed length initially (256, 512, 1024, 2048) based on the time budget.\n\n2. Initialization:\n   - Start with a population of candidate sequences of length 256.\n   - Each sequence is created by taking a random permutation of 256 values, then scaling them to sum to 1 and clamping values to ensure non-negativity.\n\n3. Selection:\n   - Select parents based on their fitness scores (lower score is better).\n   - Use tournament selection to choose two parents for crossover.\n\n4. Crossover:\n   - Perform a uniform crossover between two parent sequences.\n   - Introduce small random variations during crossover to maintain diversity.\n\n5. Mutation:\n   - Apply small random mutations to the offspring with a probability of 1%.\n   - Use bounded mutations to keep values non-negative and within the allowed range.\n\n6. Evaluation:\n   - Evaluate each candidate using the `evaluate_sequence` function.\n   - The fitness function is based on the inverse of the score (lower fitness means higher score, worse solution).\n\n7. Replacement:\n   - Keep the best candidate from the previous generation to ensure progressive improvement.\n   - Replace the worst candidates in the new generation with the best ones.\n\n8. Population Management:\n   - Maintain a population size of 500 (adjustable if constraints are hit).\n   - Each generation will have 250 children (500 parents, but we use tournament selection for 2 parents per child).\n\n9. Termination Conditions:\n   - Iterate for 1500 seconds total.\n   - Terminate if we find a candidate that improves upon the previous best score by a certain factor (1.5%).\n   - The best candidate found during the search will be saved and returned.\n\n10. Steps:\n\n    - **Phase 1**: Warm start with the provided parent sequence (if available) as one of the initial candidates.\n    - **Phase 2**: Perform crossover and mutation operations on the population.\n    - **Phase 3**: Evaluate each candidate using the exact objective function.\n    - **Phase 4**: Repeat until time is up.\n\n11. Hyperparameter Tuning:\n\n    - **Mutation Rate**: 1%\n    - **Population Size**: Starts at 500, reduces if memory becomes too costly.\n    - **Crossover Variation**: Small random numbers centered around zero.\n\n12. Code Structure:\n\n    We'll implement a time-aware GA loop that balances between rounds and exact evaluation. We'll make sure to avoid floating-point precision issues and use defensive copies where necessary.\n\n    We'll define:\n        - A function to initialize a population.\n        - Fitness evaluation functions.\n        - Crossover and mutation functions.\n\n    The central optimization function will be `propose_candidate_ga`, which uses a genetic algorithm approach to find the minimal score.",
